# Commit Messages

**Whenever a commit message is requested, always give both versions.**

Output them in this order, each in its own code block:

1. **Detailed** — the subject line, a blank line, then a body explaining what
   changed and why, then the co-author trailer.
2. **Short** — the subject line, a blank line, then the co-author trailer. No body.

Only output the message. Never run `git commit` unless explicitly asked to.

## Scope of changes

**Default to the most recent (incremental) change only** — the delta since the last
commit-message request, not the whole accumulated working-tree diff. Cover the full or
cumulative diff only when the user explicitly asks for "all changes" (or similar).

## Subject line

```
TNS-XXXX type(scope): subject
```

- `TNS-XXXX` — the Jira ticket, taken from the current branch name. If the branch
  has no ticket, ask for one; drop the prefix only if there genuinely isn't one.
- `type` — Conventional Commits: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `chore`, `docs`, `test`.
- `(scope)` — optional; a feature area such as `(Turbo Live)`, `(api)`, `(sidebar)`.
- `subject` — English, imperative mood, no trailing period.

## Co-author trailer

Every message Claude writes ends, after a blank line, with a trailer naming the
model that actually did the work:

```
Co-Authored-By: Claude <model> <noreply@anthropic.com>
```

`<model>` is the model running the session, written the way it names itself —
`Opus 4.8 (1M context)`, `Sonnet 5`, `Haiku 4.5`, and so on. Never copy the model
from the examples below; read it off the current session. Different sessions use
different models, so the trailer differs commit to commit — that is expected.

Where the harness prescribes an exact trailer line for the session's model, use
that line verbatim.

## Example

```
TNS-1931 feat(rubric): unify the grading-rubric entry as name + weights

The rubric entry was duplicated across the table modal and the detail page,
so the two drifted apart. Both now render the same component.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <model> <noreply@anthropic.com>
```

```
TNS-1931 feat(rubric): unify the grading-rubric entry as name + weights

Co-Authored-By: Claude <model> <noreply@anthropic.com>
```

## One commit covering several changes

This is the common case. Keep it to a single subject line — do not emit one
message per change.

- The subject states the overall intent the changes share, not a list of them.
  If they share no intent, say so and suggest splitting the commit.
- The type is the most significant one across the changes (`feat` beats `fix`,
  `fix` beats `refactor`/`chore`). Drop the scope when they span several areas.
- The body lists one bullet per change, each naming what changed and why.
- The short version stays a bare subject line, with no bullets.

```
TNS-1940 feat: return to the original page from the maintenance page

- redirectOnOutage now passes the current path as a `from` query param so the
  maintenance page knows where the user came from
- maintenance.html sends the back button to `from` when it is a safe
  same-origin path, and falls back to `/` otherwise
- stop redirecting on `cannot-connect`, which fires on the client's own
  network drops rather than a real backend outage

Co-Authored-By: Claude <model> <noreply@anthropic.com>
```

## Reverts

Keep the git convention — subject is `Revert "<original subject>"`, body says why
it is being reverted.

## Breaking changes

Mark the type with `!` and add a footer describing the migration:

```
TNS-1950 feat(api)!: require an account id on every room request

BREAKING CHANGE: callRoomApi callers must now pass accountId; requests without
it are rejected with 400.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <model> <noreply@anthropic.com>
```
